WEBVTT

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:03.040
Progress measurement keeps deliberate practice honest.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:03.890
Without measurement,

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:07.420
practice can become a ritual that consumes time without improving capability.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.470
A baseline shows where performance starts.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:13.600
Metrics, rubrics, examples of good work, decision records, practice logs,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.170
and before-and-after comparisons can all help show whether the learner is actually

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:17.760
improving.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.980
Good measurement includes both qualitative and quantitative evidence.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:25.200
A quantitative measure might count time to complete a task, number of defects found,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.240
number of strong hypotheses generated, or score on a rubric.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.800
Qualitative evidence might include better reasoning, clearer explanations,

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.715
stronger prioritization, or fewer repeated mistakes.

00:00:34.715 --> 00:00:37.080
The right measure depends on the skill being practiced.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.675
Calibration is part of progress.

00:00:38.675 --> 00:00:41.920
A learner should understand how confidence compares to actual performance.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.270
If confidence rises faster than capability, practice may be creating false certainty.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.000
If capability improves but confidence stays too low,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.080
the learner may not use the skill when needed.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.600
Decision records and review notes help learners compare what they expected with what

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.500
actually happened.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.750
Avoid vanity metrics.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.560
Hours practiced, pages read, or tasks completed may be useful context,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.720
but they do not prove skill growth by themselves.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.340
The core question is whether real capability improved.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.360
Measurement should help decide whether to continue the current practice,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.320
adjust the target, seek better feedback,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.440
or move to the next subskill because the current bottleneck has improved.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.560
A good progress review should lead to a decision.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.920
Keep the same drill if the learner is still improving.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.515
Change the drill if the measure is no longer connected to real performance.

00:01:21.515 --> 00:01:23.950
Increase difficulty if the task has become too easy.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.790
Return to a simpler component if errors show that the foundation is not stable yet.
